ata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.374695Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.375557Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.418401Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.375501Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.417091Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Euclidean Distance: 0.7404513\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":144},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Dot Product\n\nDot product considers both direction and magnitude.\n\nIt is often used when the embedding model and retrieval setup are designed for inner-product search.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"dot_product = np.dot(A, B)\n\nprint(\"Dot Product:\", dot_product)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.420904Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.421404Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.445231Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.421354Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-02T13:00:50.443365Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Dot Product: 0.7258659\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":145},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Comparison of similarity algorithms\n\n| Metric             | What it measures        | Best score |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------- | ---------- |\n| Cosine similarity  | Direction similarity    | Higher     |\n| Euclidean distance | Straight line distance  | Lower      |\n| Dot product        | Direction and magnitude | Higher     |\n\nImportant production consideration:\n\nYou should choose the metric according to the embedding model's training and retrieval recommendations.\n\nFor normalized vectors, cosine similarity and dot product produce equivalent rankings because the vector magnitudes are 1.\n\nLikewise, for unitnormalized vectors:\n\n$$\n||A-B||^2=2-2(A \\cdot B)\n$$\n\nThis means maximizing cosine similarity, maximizing dot product, and minimizing squared Euclidean distance give the same ranking.\n\n![Similarity Algorithm](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/cosine_similarity_and_dot_product.png)\n\n## Differences Between Embedding Models\n\nIf TF-IDF and embedding models both convert text into vectors.\n\nThe most important difference is:\n\n- TF-IDF vectorization represents the importance of words in a document.\n- Embedding vectorization represents the semantic meaning of a piece of text.\n\nBoth convert text into numerical vectors, and both can use cosine similarity. However, the vectors represent completely different types of information.\n\nLet's understand this deeply with practical examples.\n\n### TF-IDF Vectorization (Keyword Search)\n\nIt is a statistical technique that converts text into numerical vectors based on the words that appear in the text and how informative those words are across a collection of documents.\n\n**Example**\n\nSuppose we have three documents:\n\n- Document 1: I love machine learning\n- Document 2: I love deep learning\n- Document 3: I love artificial intelligence\n\nFirst, we build a vocabulary from the corpus:\n\nVocabulary\n\n`[I, love, machine, learning, deep, artificial, intelligence]`\n\nTF-IDF assigns a numerical value to each vocabulary term for each document.\n\nTF-IDF values in Document 1\n\n$$[0.1, 0.1, 0.7, 0.5, 0, 0, 0]$$\n\nWhat does this vector mean?\n\n- `machine` has a high weight.\n- `learning` has a moderate weight.\n- `deep`, `artificial`, and `intelligence` have zero weight in this document.\n- The vector is based on the vocabulary and the statistical properties of the corpus.\n\nImportant: TF-IDF does not understand what machine learning means. It only calculates the statistical importance of the terms.\n\n### Embedding Vectorization (Semantic Search)\n\nAn embedding model is a neural network trained to transform text into a numerical vector that captures semantic information.\n\nExamples of embedding models:\n\n- `sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`\n- `text-embedding-3-small` (OpenAI)\n- `text-embedding-3-large` (OpenAI)\n- `BGE`\n- `E5`\n\nUnlike TF-IDF, embeddings do not simply assign weights to vocabulary words.\n\nThey learn a representation in which texts with similar meanings tend to have vectors that are close to one another.\n\n**Example**\n\nSuppose we have a documents. We pass each document through an embedding model.\n\nAn embedding vector might look conceptually like this:\n\n```\nDocument 1:[0.021, -0.134, 0.087, 0.452, ..., -0.091]\n```\n\nNotice something important.\n\nThese numbers do not directly represent the weights of the words `I`, `love`, `machine`, or `learning`.\n\nInstead, the dimensions collectively encode learned semantic features. Individual dimensions are usually not directly interpretable as specific words or concepts.\n\nFor example, the embedding model may represent these sentences as semantically similar:\n\n- \"I love machine learning.\"\n- \"I enjoy studying artificial intelligence.\"\n\nEven though many of their words are different.\n\n### The Fundamental Differences\n\nLet's compare them directly.\n\n| Property               | TF-IDF Vector                          | Embedding Vector                                         |\n| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Technique              | Statistical                            | Neural network                                           |\n| Purpose                | Represent word importance              | Represent semantic meaning                               |\n| Word importance        | Explicitly calculated                  | Learned indirectly                                       |\n| Vocabulary             | Requires a corpus vocabulary           | Uses a trained model's tokenizer                         |\n| Vector dimensions      | Usually vocabulary size                | Fixed model dimension                                    |\n| Vector type            | Usually sparse                         | Usually dense                                            |\n| Semantic understanding | No inherent understanding              | Learns semantic relationships                            |\n| Synonyms               | Usually not recognized as equivalent   | Often represented as similar                             |\n| Context                | Limited                                | Can capture context, depending on model                  |\n| New words              | Requires vocabulary handling           | Model tokenizer can handle many unseen words as subwords |\n| Training               | No neural training required for TF-IDF | Model is pretrained or trained                           |\n| Storage                | Sparse vectors are efficient           | Dense vectors typically need more storage                |\n\nLet's focus on three particularly important differences.\n\n#### Sparse vector vs. dense vector\n\n**TF-IDF**\n\n- Sparse vector: most dimensions may be zero.\n\nFor TF-IDF:\n\n```\n[0, 0, 0.8, 0, 0.4, 0, 0, 0]\n```\n\n**Embedding**\n\n- Dense vector: most dimensions contain values.\n\nFor an embedding:\n\n```\n[0.12, -0.31, 0.08, 0.44, -0.21, 0.19, ...]\n```\n\nTF-IDF vector dimensions correspond to terms in the vocabulary.\n\nEmbedding dimensions correspond to learned numerical features.\n\n#### Exact word matching vs. semantic matching\n\nSuppose your database contains:\n\n```\nDocument A: I drive a car.\nDocument B: I drive an automobile.\nDocument C: I enjoy eating pizza.\n```\n\nYour search query is:\n\n```\nI drive a car.\n```\n\n##### TF-IDF keyword search\n\n**Document A**\n\n- Strong match\n- Exact terms\n\n**Document B**\n\n- May score lower\n- Different word\n\n**Document C**\n\n- Low match\n- Different meaning\n\nTF-IDF mainly relies on shared vocabulary. Because `car` and `automobile` are different terms, TF-IDF doesn't inherently know they mean the same thing.\n\n##### Embedding semantic search\n\n**Document A**\n\n- Very similar\n- Same words + meaning\n\n**Document B**\n\n- Likely similar\n- Similar meaning\n\n**Document C**\n\n- Less similar\n- Different meaning\n\nAn embedding model trained to capture semantic similarity may recognize that `car` and `automobile` are related.\n\nThis is why semantic search can retrieve relevant documents even when the query and document use different words.\n\n#### What does cosine similarity actually measure?\n\n\nThe difference is the meaning of the input vectors.\n\nCosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors:\n\nBut what are those vectors representing?\n\n**TF-IDF + Cosine**\n\n- Measures similarity in word-weight space\n- How much do these documents share important terms?\n\n**Embedding + Cosine**\n\n- Measures similarity in semantic space\n- How similar are the meanings of these texts according to the embedding model?\n\nThe cosine similarity formula is the same. The vectorization technique is different.\n\n# Metadata Filtering\n\nMetadata filtering lets you restrict vector search to records that satisfy particular conditions.\n\nImagine you have 1 million documents from different departments:\n\n```\n{\n    \"text\": \"Employee salary information...\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"department\": \"HR\",\n        \"year\": 2025,\n        \"access_level\": \"private\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAnother document:\n\n```\n{\n    \"text\": \"Python programming tutorial...\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"department\": \"Engineering\",\n        \"year\": 2026,\n        \"access_level\": \"public\"\n    }\n}\n```\n\nNow the user asks:\n\n> Find Python documentation from Engineering.\n\nYou can apply:\n\n```py\nfilter = {    \n  \"department\": \"Engineering\",    \n  \"access_level\": \"public\"\n}\n```\n\nThe database performs vector retrieval with the relevant metadata constraints.\n\n**Why is this important?**\n\nWithout filtering, the database might return:\n\n- Documents from unrelated departments.\n- Outdated information.\n- Documents the user shouldn't access.\n- Irrelevant categories.\n\nMetadata filtering is therefore important for both relevance and security. It applied before vector similarity search.\n\nProduction warning: Metadata filters are not a replacement for authorization. Your application must enforce access permissions using trusted user identity and server-side policy. Never trust a user-provided filter as the only access-control mechanism.\n\n# Hybrid Search\n\nImagine a user searches:\n\n> What is the error code ERR-502 in FastAPI?\n\nThe exact error code matters.\n\nA semantic search system might retrieve documents discussing HTTP errors generally but fail to prioritize the exact string.\n\nThis is where hybrid search becomes useful.\n\nHybrid search combines:\n\n1. Sparse or keyword search.\n2. Dense vector search.\n\n## Sparse vector search\n\nSparse vectors contain mostly zero values, with only selected dimensions having nonzero values.\n\nA traditional example is a bag-of-words or TF IDF representation.\n\nSparse retrieval can be particularly useful for exact terms, technical identifiers, and rare keywords.\n\nA popular keyword based retrieval algorithm is BM25.\n\n## Dense vector search\n\nDense vectors are embeddings containing many numerical dimensions, typically with most dimensions nonzero.\n\nThey are useful for semantic similarity.\n\n## How hybrid search works\n\n![Hybrid Search Pipeline](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/handbook/main/static/img/ai-engineering/hybrid-search-pipeline.png)\n\nOne common fusion technique is Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF).\n\nIts formula is:\n\n$$ RRF(d)=\\sum{r\\in R} \\frac{1}{k+rank_r(d)} $$\n\nWhere:\n\n- $d$ = document.\n- $R$ = different ranked result lists.\n- $rank_r(d)$ = document rank in a result list.\n- $k$ = a smoothing constant.\n\nThe system combines the rankings from different retrieval methods.\n\nWhen should you use hybrid search?\n\n- Technical documentation.\n- Legal documents.\n- Product catalogs.\n- Code search.\n- Enterprise knowledge bases.\n- RAG applications containing exact IDs or technical terminology.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null}]}